### ARTICLE 6 — LIMITED USE WITHOUT A LOSDA

**6.1 Purpose of this Article.** This Article defines the narrow envelope in which a Work may
be executed without a LOSDA. It exists so that the public can verify and learn from the Work
without making the framework unworkable. It is not a general permission to use the Work.

**6.2 Mode.** The Author specifies the applicable Mode in the NOTICE:
(a) **Mode S (Standard)** — Evaluation Use is permitted under Section 6.3;
(b) **Mode U (Universal)** — no execution of any kind is permitted without a LOSDA; Section
6.3 does not apply. If the NOTICE is silent, Mode S applies.

**6.3 Evaluation Use.** In Mode S, and without a LOSDA, You may execute the Work solely for
Evaluation Use, provided that all of the following are true:
(a) the execution is private to You and does not serve any person other than You;
(b) the execution is not connected to real users, real customers, real production data, or
any live system;
(c) the execution is not part of any Commercial Deployment and generates no revenue;
(d) the execution is not part of any product, service, or offering made available to others;
(e) the execution does not exceed thirty (30) cumulative days per Work per twelve-month
period, unless the Author states a longer period in the NOTICE;
(f) the execution is not used to develop a competing product or a substitute for the Work;
(g) You retain all notices and do not remove attribution from the Work's interface or
outputs where present.

**6.4 Evaluation Use is not a deployment license.** Evaluation Use creates no expectation of
continuity, confers no right to support, and does not survive the termination of this
License.

**6.5 Extension.** The Author or the Steward may issue an Evaluation Extension LOSDA under
Appendix A to extend the period in Section 6.3(e) or to widen the envelope for a defined
purpose.

**6.6 Organizational limit.** Evaluation Use by an entity is limited to a single copy per
Work for the entity as a whole, and may not be used to evaluate the Work in a manner that
substitutes for production use across an organization.

---

### ARTICLE 7 — NON-COMMERCIAL RESEARCH, TEACHING, AND STUDY EXCEPTION

**7.1 Research use.** Accredited educational institutions, public research organizations, and
independent researchers may execute a Work for Non-Commercial Purpose in a research setting
without a LOSDA, provided that:
(a) the execution is confined to the researcher's controlled environment;
(b) no real personal data of third parties is processed without a lawful basis;
(c) results are published in a manner that does not disclose confidential information;
(d) the execution does not provide a service to persons outside the research team;
(e) the execution is not used to train or improve a commercial product; and
(f) the researcher acknowledges the Work and this License in resulting publications.

**7.2 Teaching use.** An instructor may execute a Work in a classroom or laboratory setting
for teaching purposes without a LOSDA, provided that the execution is confined to the
teaching environment, is not offered as a service, and is not used for the institution's
operational or administrative needs.

**7.3 Institutional deployment requires a LOSDA.** Deployment of a Work in an institution's
production systems, including student information systems, learning platforms, libraries,
administrative systems, and research infrastructure serving users at scale, requires a
LOSDA. An Academic LOSDA is available at no fee under Appendix A.

**7.4 Attribution.** Published research, teaching material, and theses that rely on the Work
must cite the Work, its Authors, and this License.

---

### ARTICLE 8 — SECURITY RESEARCH AND COORDINATED DISCLOSURE

**8.1 Safe harbor.** Good-faith security research conducted under this Article does not
constitute a Violation and does not breach any anti-circumvention term of this License.

**8.2 Conditions.** The safe harbor applies where the researcher:
(a) accesses only systems and data that the researcher is authorized to access, or that are
provided by the Author for testing;
(b) does not degrade, disrupt, or deny service to others;
(c) does not exfiltrate, retain, or publish personal data or trade secrets;
(d) reports the vulnerability privately to the Author or the Steward without undue delay,
and in any event before public disclosure;
(e) allows a coordinated disclosure period of ninety (90) days, extendable by mutual written
agreement, or such shorter period as the Author permits;
(f) does not use the vulnerability for extortion, advantage, or commercial exploitation; and
(g) complies with applicable law.

**8.3 No liability for research.** Subject to Section 8.2, the Author waives claims under this
License arising from good-faith security research.

**8.4 Disclosure of license violations.** Reporting a suspected Violation to the Steward is a
protected act. The Steward must not disclose the identity of a reporter without consent,
except where required by law.

**8.5 Vulnerability handling.** The Author or Steward must acknowledge a report within seven
(7) days and must publish a remediation plan within thirty (30) days, or explain why none is
possible.

---

### ARTICLE 9 — PUBLIC-INTEREST AND HUMANITARIAN USES

**9.1 Emergency use.** During a declared public emergency, a governmental or humanitarian
organization may Deploy a Work without prior LOSDA for the duration of the emergency, up to
ninety (90) days, provided that it (a) notifies the Author or Steward in writing within
fourteen (14) days of commencing Deployment, (b) does not sell the Deployment, and (c)
applies for a regular LOSDA within the ninety-day period if Deployment is to continue.

**9.2 Continuity of essential services.** Where a Deployment supports an essential service
and the Author becomes unavailable, insolvent, or unresponsive for more than sixty (60) days,
the deployer may apply to the Steward for a Continuity LOSDA, which the Steward may issue on
behalf of the Author in accordance with the Registry Policy.

**9.3 Public-interest licensing commitment.** The Steward must maintain a Public-Interest
Program under which LOSDAs are issued at no fee for qualifying uses, including disaster
response, public health, accessibility, civil-liberties tooling, and digital public
infrastructure.

**9.4 Non-discrimination.** LOSDAs must not be refused on the basis of nationality, ethnicity,
religion, sex, gender identity, sexual orientation, disability, age, caste, or political
opinion, except where refusal is required by law or by sanctions.

---

### ARTICLE 10 — THE LETTER OF SOFTWARE DEPLOYMENT APPROVAL (LOSDA)

**10.1 Function.** A LOSDA is the only instrument by which the Author grants the right to
Deploy a Work. A LOSDA operates within this License and does not replace it. The licensee
remains bound by this License in addition to the LOSDA.

**10.2 Issuance authority.** Only the Author may issue a LOSDA for a Work. The Steward may
issue a LOSDA only where (a) the Author has expressly appointed the Steward as its issuing
agent in the NOTICE; (b) the LOSDA is a Continuity LOSDA under Section 9.2; or (c) the LOSDA
is an Evaluation Extension under Section 6.5 and the Author has not objected in writing.

**10.3 Form.** A LOSDA must be issued in the form published at `templates/LOSDA-TEMPLATE.md`,
must contain every element listed in Appendix A, and must be recorded in the LOSDA Registry
before it takes effect.

**10.4 No valid LOSDA without registration.** A LOSDA that is not recorded in the Registry,
or whose record does not match the instrument presented, is not valid. A Registry entry that
has been superseded, suspended, or revoked confers no permission.

**10.5 Scope is exhaustive.** A LOSDA grants only what it expressly states. Rights not stated
are not granted. Any ambiguity is resolved against expansion of scope.

**10.6 Non-exclusive.** Unless the LOSDA states otherwise, a LOSDA is non-exclusive and does
not prevent the Author from issuing other LOSDAs for the same Work.

**10.7 Non-transferable.** A LOSDA is personal to the licensee named in it, unless it names
Affiliates, successors, or assignees. Assignment requires the Author's prior written
approval, recorded in the Registry.

**10.8 Sublicensing.** A LOSDA does not permit sublicensing unless it expressly does so.

**10.9 Fees.** Fees, if any, are stated in the LOSDA. Fees are for permission to Deploy, not
for copies of the Work, and are not a sale of the Work or of any rights in it. Fee terms,
refund policy, and payment schedule must be stated or incorporated by reference.

**10.10 Term.** A LOSDA must state its term, or state that it is perpetual subject to
compliance. Where a term is stated, Deployment after expiry is a Violation unless renewed.

**10.11 Amendment.** A LOSDA may be amended only by a written instrument issued by the
issuing authority and recorded in the Registry. Amendments that reduce the licensee's rights
take effect only with the licensee's written consent.

**10.12 Verification.** Every LOSDA carries a Verification URL. A deployer must be able to
produce, on request, its LOSDA ID and a current Registry status.

**10.13 Public summary.** The Registry must publish, for each LOSDA, the LOSDA ID, the
Licensed Work, the LOSDA Class, the licensee's public display name (or an anonymized
identifier at the licensee's request where the policy permits), the Effective Date, the term,
the Territory, and the status.

**10.14 Classes.** LOSDAs are issued in the following classes, further described in
`legal/losda-specification.md` and `docs/deployment-scopes.md`:
(a) **S — Standard Commercial**; (b) **E — Enterprise / Portfolio**; (c) **I — Internal
Use**; (d) **H — Hosted Service / SaaS**; (e) **D — Distribution / OEM / Embedded**;
(f) **A — Academic / Non-Commercial**; (g) **G — Government / Public Sector**;
(h) **P — Public Interest**; (i) **R — Research**; (j) **X — Evaluation Extension**;
(k) **C — Continuity**; (l) **M — Machine Learning / AI Data**; (m) **O — Open Deployment**
(authorized public deployment, where the Author chooses to permit it).

**10.15 Revocation.** A LOSDA may be revoked only for cause under Section 18.7, subject to the
Reliance Period in Article 19.

---

### ARTICLE 11 — OBLIGATIONS OF A DEPLOYMENT LICENSEE

A licensee under a LOSDA must:

**11.1 Deploy within scope.** Deploy only within the Deployment Scope, and obtain an amended
or additional LOSDA before expanding scope, including by increased scale, new Territory, new
Field of Use, new Affiliates, new products, or new hosting arrangements.

**11.2 Preserve notices.** Preserve all copyright, license, attribution, and NOTICE material
in the Deployment, in the Source Code, and in documentation provided to users.

**11.3 Identify the license to users.** Where the Deployment exposes an interface to end
users, present the license identification, the Author's attribution, and a link to the
canonical repository in a reasonably accessible location.

**11.4 Report scale.** Report Deployment scale at the intervals stated in the LOSDA, where
the LOSDA contains scale limits.

**11.5 Security cooperation.** Notify the Author without undue delay of any material security
incident arising from or affecting the Deployment, and cooperate in good faith on remediation.

**11.6 No circumvention.** Not structure its affairs to avoid the need for a LOSDA, including
by splitting an organization, interposing an Affiliate, using a contractor as a conduit,
or re-hosting.

**11.7 No hostile assertions.** Not assert any patent, copyright, or other right against the
Author or any Contributor in respect of the Work, except in defense of an assertion first
made against the licensee.

**11.8 Compliance.** Comply with the laws applicable to the Deployment, including data
protection, consumer protection, accessibility, and sector regulation.

**11.9 Records.** Keep records sufficient to demonstrate compliance with this Article for
three (3) years after the end of the term, and make them available to the Author on
reasonable request, subject to confidentiality.

**11.10 Accurate registry data.** Provide accurate licensee identification and keep contact
details current.

