### ARTICLE 19 — RELIANCE PROTECTION AND WIND-DOWN

**19.1 Purpose.** This Article protects licensees who have invested in Deployment in good
reliance on a valid LOSDA. It does not protect a licensee that is in breach.

**19.2 Reliance Period.** Where a LOSDA terminates or is revoked for a reason other than the
licensee's material breach, sanctions, or a court order, the licensee may continue the
existing Deployment for one hundred and eighty (180) days, or for the remainder of the stated
term if shorter, provided that the licensee:
(a) is not in material breach;
(b) pays any accrued fees;
(c) uses the period solely to migrate away from the Work or to negotiate a replacement
LOSDA; and
(d) does not expand the Deployment during the period.

**19.3 No new deployments.** During the Reliance Period the licensee must not commence new
Deployments, onboard new users beyond ordinary growth, or enter new Fields of Use.

**19.4 Perpetual grants.** Where a LOSDA states that it is perpetual and irrevocable, the
Author may revoke it only for (a) the licensee's material breach, (b) fraud, (c) a requirement
of law, or (d) the grounds in Section 18.7(c).

**19.5 Release-based certainty.** Once a version of a Work has been released under a given
version of this License, the Author must not revoke the rights granted under Article 3 in
respect of that released version.

**19.6 Wind-down support.** The Author should provide reasonable migration assistance during
a Reliance Period where the Author terminated the LOSDA without cause.

