### ARTICLE 20 — MONITORING, AUDIT, AND ENFORCEMENT

**20.1 Good-faith enforcement.** The Author and the Steward must exercise enforcement powers
in good faith, proportionately, and consistently, and must publish enforcement statistics
annually.

**20.2 Detection.** The Author or Steward may investigate suspected Violations using publicly
available information, reports, and, where the licensee has consented in the LOSDA, audit
rights. Investigation must not involve unauthorized access to systems.

**20.3 Reporting a Violation.** Any person may report a suspected Violation using the form in
`templates/VIOLATION-REPORT.md` or the repository's issue template. Reports may be made
confidentially on request.

**20.4 Notice of Non-Compliance.** On credible evidence of a Violation, the Author or Steward
must issue an NNC stating (a) the identity of the Work; (b) the conduct alleged; (c) the
provision breached; (d) the evidence relied on, to the extent disclosure is lawful; (e) the
cure period; and (f) the consequences of non-compliance.

**20.5 Cure period.** The standard cure period is thirty (30) days. For non-commercial,
good-faith, or accidental breaches, the cure period is extended to sixty (60) days where the
breaching party engages in good faith. For breaches causing harm to persons, the cure period
may be shortened to seven (7) days.

**20.6 Enforcement ladder.** Enforcement must proceed in the following order, escalating only
as necessary:
(a) **Stage 0 — Informal resolution:** a private message inviting correction;
(b) **Stage 1 — NNC and cure:** formal notice and cure opportunity;
(c) **Stage 2 — Suspension:** suspension of LOSDA or of the Visibility Grant in respect of the
breaching party;
(d) **Stage 3 — Revocation and Registry entry:** revocation of LOSDA, public redacted entry in
the Enforcement Register;
(e) **Stage 4 — Platform action:** takedown requests, package registry complaints, app store
complaints, and hosting notifications;
(f) **Stage 5 — Legal action:** civil proceedings, including claims for infringement, breach,
and injunctive relief, and, where the conduct is criminal under applicable law, referral to
authorities.

**20.7 Proportionality.** Enforcement must be proportionate to (a) the seriousness of the
breach, (b) whether the breach was knowing, (c) the harm caused, (d) the breaching party's
size and capacity, and (e) the breaching party's cooperation.

**20.8 No private enforcement beyond the framework.** Beneficiaries may report Violations and
may exercise rights as third-party beneficiaries under Section 25.11, but must not demand
payment, threaten publicity, or purport to grant or withhold LOSDAs.

**20.9 Enforcement Register.** The Steward must maintain the Enforcement Register containing,
for each action, the date, the LOSDA ID or Work, the provision breached, the stage reached, the
outcome, and the appeal status. Personal data must be minimized and redacted consistently
with Article 23.

**20.10 Misuse of enforcement.** Knowingly false reports are a Violation and may result in
restriction of the reporter's participation in the community, subject to appeal.

**20.11 Presumption of good faith.** A party that has cured a breach in full is presumed to be
in compliance unless new evidence arises.

**20.12 Limitation.** Enforcement action must be commenced within the period permitted by
applicable law. Failure to enforce in one instance is not a waiver.

