### ARTICLE 24 — GOVERNING LAW, DISPUTE RESOLUTION, AND JURISDICTION

**24.1 Governing law.** This License is governed by the laws of the People's Republic of
Bangladesh, without regard to conflict-of-laws rules, unless the NOTICE or the LOSDA
designates another governing law for that Work or that LOSDA. Mandatory consumer-protection
and employment rules of the licensee's habitual residence prevail where they cannot be
contractually displaced.

**24.2 Amicable settlement.** The parties must attempt in good faith to resolve any dispute
arising out of or relating to this License or a LOSDA through negotiation for thirty (30)
days after written notice of the dispute.

**24.3 Mediation.** If negotiation fails, the parties must attempt mediation administered by a
recognized mediation institution for a further thirty (30) days, unless the dispute concerns
urgent injunctive relief or a security incident.

**24.4 Arbitration.** Failing settlement, the dispute must be finally resolved by arbitration
administered under the UNCITRAL Arbitration Rules, with a seat in Dhaka, Bangladesh, before
one arbitrator, in the English language. Where the parties are outside Bangladesh, they may
agree in the LOSDA to a different seat or to institutional arbitration. The award is final and
binding, and judgment on the award may be entered in any court of competent jurisdiction
subject to applicable public policy.

**24.5 Small claims and consumer disputes.** Either party may bring an individual claim in a
court of competent jurisdiction where the value of the claim is below the applicable
small-claims threshold, or where mandatory law grants a forum to a consumer.

**24.6 Injunctive relief.** Nothing in this Article prevents either party from seeking urgent
interim or injunctive relief from a court of competent jurisdiction, including relief to
prevent irreparable harm, to protect confidential information, or to prevent a security
incident.

**24.7 Enforcement actions by the Steward.** The Steward may bring a claim in its own name as
steward of the framework where the claim concerns the integrity of the Registry, the
Enforcement Register, or the use of the Trademarks.

**24.8 No class proceedings waiver where prohibited.** Nothing in this Article prevents
participation in a class or representative proceeding where applicable law grants that right,
nor does it prevent a claim by a consumer body acting under statute.

**24.9 Language.** Arbitration and dispute proceedings are conducted in English unless the
parties agree otherwise.

**24.10 Continuity.** If the arbitration agreement is held unenforceable, the courts of Dhaka,
Bangladesh, have exclusive jurisdiction, subject to Section 24.5.

